mr general always good to see you uh  particularly this  early hour um we've got a lot to talk  about on your brief but  can we just start with the the issues  such as they may be  around vaccination why has there been an  instruction a change to the schedule  in terms of vaccination how much of a  delay do we see being built into the  system  well we do think there are going to be  some supply issues in the weeks ahead  but this isn't anything that people  should be worried about we're still on  course  to meet our targets that's to vaccinate  all of the groups one to nine by the  middle of april and all adults to  receive  their first vaccine by the end of july  nobody who has an appointment should be  concerned  you're still going to get your second  vaccine all those appointments will be  honored  but we always said right from the  beginning that a new manufacturing  process would have its lumps and bumps  and that has been the case in the past  and  i'm sure it will be in the future we're  sourcing vaccines from all over the  world and  we are experiencing occasionally some  issues and that's led to this  uh this issue with some supply in the  coming weeks but  as i say the main thing is we're still  very much on course we've still got line  of sight  to deliver the vaccines and to meet our  targets  just to be really specific and indeed on  something you just mentioned there  problems in obtaining the vaccine from  around the world there's a suggestion  this morning that perhaps the  the issue as regards the the az oxford  vaccine it is a problem in india where  where they do produce is that  is that your understanding well i'm not  going to get into  the specific contracts that we have with  different manufacturers  we're sourcing vaccines from many  countries all over the world  it's a very complex international supply  chain and as i say  that does mean occasionally we will  experience issues and and that is what  we've experienced right now but there is  no reason to be concerned  we are on course to meet our target  which is to get those first categories  of one to nine  vaccinated by the middle of april and  that was always a challenging target  remember we only met our target in  mid-february  with a couple of days grace and so the  targets the prime minister has set us  are not easy ones but we are on course  uh to  to to to achieve that one and myself  matt hancock nadeem zahawi  the government we have line of sight  that we've got enough vaccines  to do that and so that's the main thing  and that's what we're focused on right  now  indeed and of course as you as you've do  as you alluded to the health sector  yesterday in in downing street made it  very clear that those  those deadlines those targets would  would still be met there is a little bit  of concern though  on whether or not this might have some  impact uh  on the road map itself i mean is the is  the  is the map roadmap actually based on  those kind of confirmed vaccination  targets on those dates that the  the health secretary still stuck to  yesterday  well the the vaccine rollout is our  route out of this  the more people we vaccinate the easier  it will be to open up our society and  get us back to  the way of life that we all want to see  there's no reason to believe that the  roadmap  is affected by uh this temporary  shortage  in supply uh the the main thing is that  we are  on course to meet the targets of the  prime minister set us up getting uh  the categories one to nine which is all  the over 50s including the  the clinically extremely vulnerable  below that age vaccinated by  the middle of april so there's no  concern that uh we're off course with  the road capture  just one last question on the vaccine  and it is a distance from your brief so  you may well not know this  but does the madonna jab start coming  into circulation  uh in april i don't know  to be honest the the exact date that uh  the modern chap will be in use within  the uk uh obviously we have uh the  orders placed  and uh we'll be using them as quickly as  they they come into the country  i actually apolo apologies that  secretaries did i i i  inadvertently misspoke that one more  question on on the vaccines  what what do you make of the the saber  rattling that we're seeing from amongst  others ursula wonderland  the the threat that somehow that the the  eu could have a rather spurious  interpretation to my eyes at least of  article 22  and intervene in vaccinations and  delivery of vaccines that we have  bought that we have paid for that are on  contractual basis hours  that the eu might step in to intervene  there  well i was surprised and disappointed by  those comments but the prime minister  had spoken earlier in the year to ursula  vanderlian and she gave a very clear  commitment  which was that uh the eu would not  engage in this sort of activity  that contractual responsibilities would  be honoured  and that's exactly what we intend to do  and i i hope and expect  the eu to to stick to their side of the  bargain as well it isn't at all helpful  if we enter into this sort of  conversation that's not the way the uk  is behaving or wants to behave in the  future we all need to work together  to get through this supplies of vaccines  rely on very complex  international manufacturing supply  chains as we've just said  and so everybody needs to uh to have  free trade  vaccines and not get into this sort of  this sort of difficulty  um talk to us a little bit then about  about rough sleeping the rough sleeping  accommodation program of course did  serve to take thousands of people off of  the streets at a time when  you know everyone was very very  concerned about the spread of the  pandemic we certainly knew a lot less  and  we're moving on to the second stage of  that now  that's right a year ago uh almost to the  day we launched everyone in which  had a simple objective of asking those  people who are sleeping rough on the  streets are most vulnerable to covered  19  to come into safe and secure  accommodation it was hugely successful  we ended up bringing in over 37 000  people who are either sleeping on the  streets or in otherwise pretty  precarious situations like sofa surfing  with friends  and relatives most of those people have  now been able to move on into better  accommodation  either social housing the private rental  sector or what's called move on  accommodation  which is temporary with accommodation  with wrap around care  about 27 000 people have been given that  sort of help over the course of the last  year  but we still got many people in  temporary emergency accommodation  and today we're putting in place further  support  so that we can build up good quality  accommodation to help those people to  access it and begin to rebuild their  lives we've secured over 400 million  pounds  and today we're releasing the next 200  million of that so the  charities and local councils across the  country can invest in  up to 6 000 new move on units over the  course of this parliament and that will  give us  a new national asset of accommodation so  that if people are sleeping rough  they can be moved on given a home and  then begin to  rebuild their lives yeah get help for  substance abuse  there will be no one who is what sorry  apologies minutes we've got a finite  amount of time there will be no one  working in this sector that will not  welcome additional money of course  they will welcome additional money the  question is whether or not it goes quite  so far  quite far enough i mean the statistics  as you were mentioning there 37 and a  half thousand people  in essence helped in uh as a result of  everyone in but that  i mean that figure there is obviously  going to be a disparity but that figure  of 37 and a half thousand  is a country mile away from the  department's own estimates of  of how many rough sleepers there were in  the country it was roughly 4  300 in march 2020. 